1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,877
One question is never enough.

2
00:00:02,197 --> 00:00:07,296
Real analysts ask many, adaptively, each
chosen after seeing earlier answers.

3
00:00:07,616 --> 00:00:10,325
Maybe you spot a spike in one bin, and zoom
in.

4
00:00:10,567 --> 00:00:12,572
The paper's Theorem 1 handles this.

5
00:00:12,892 --> 00:00:17,074
Write the probability of the whole
transcript as a product, step by step.

6
00:00:17,394 --> 00:00:21,700
The analyst's choice of the next question is
the same in both worlds,

7
00:00:21,700 --> 00:00:25,756
so in the ratio it cancels, leaving one
Laplace ratio per answer.

8
00:00:26,076 --> 00:00:30,640
If you know reinforcement learning, this is
the trajectory-ratio trick:

9
00:00:30,640 --> 00:00:35,847
whatever is identical in both worlds
cancels, and the per-step log ratios add up.

10
00:00:36,083 --> 00:00:39,966
So privacy losses add up, and epsilon
behaves like a budget.

11
00:00:40,286 --> 00:00:44,232
Each answer spends part of it; once it is
spent, the curator stops.

12
00:00:44,552 --> 00:00:46,927
That is the answer to Dinur and Nissim:

13
00:00:46,927 --> 00:00:50,825
the budget makes the limit on questions
explicit and measurable.

14
00:00:52,367 --> 00:00:54,778
You can now compute a real saving yourself.

15
00:00:55,098 --> 00:00:58,724
Pause: a histogram with d bins, total budget
epsilon.

16
00:00:59,044 --> 00:01:03,140
Treat each bin as its own counting query and
split the budget evenly.

17
00:01:03,460 --> 00:01:05,337
How much noise does each bin get?

18
00:01:18,183 --> 00:01:22,855
Each bin gets one over d of the budget, so
noise of scale d over epsilon.

19
00:01:23,175 --> 00:01:25,985
Treated as one query with sensitivity two,

20
00:01:25,985 --> 00:01:29,063
each bin gets two over epsilon, whatever d
is.

21
00:01:29,383 --> 00:01:34,230
The earlier framework's sharper analysis got
this down to about the square root of d,

22
00:01:34,230 --> 00:01:35,655
but it still grew with d.

23
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:39,368
Sensitivity reaches far beyond counts.

24
00:01:39,688 --> 00:01:41,459
Three examples from the paper.

25
00:01:41,767 --> 00:01:47,015
First, picture a social network where each
possible link is one row of the database.

26
00:01:47,335 --> 00:01:50,194
How many links must you cut to split the
network in two?

27
00:01:50,514 --> 00:01:55,676
That minimum cut moves by at most one when
one link changes, so it is one-sensitive.

28
00:01:55,996 --> 00:01:58,075
In general, any question of the form,

29
00:01:58,075 --> 00:02:02,737
how many rows would you have to change to
make something true, has sensitivity one.

30
00:02:02,983 --> 00:02:07,250
Second: if an algorithm that rarely looks at
any particular row,

31
00:02:07,250 --> 00:02:10,183
like one working from a small random sample,

32
00:02:10,183 --> 00:02:13,383
approximates f to within sigma most of the
time,

33
00:02:13,383 --> 00:02:17,383
on every database, then f has sensitivity at
most two sigma.

34
00:02:17,703 --> 00:02:18,791
That is Lemma 1.

35
00:02:19,033 --> 00:02:22,471
Third, the answer need not be a number: a
ranking, a set,

36
00:02:22,471 --> 00:02:26,030
a string of bits, anything with a distance
between answers.

37
00:02:26,350 --> 00:02:29,000
Pick an output with probability that decays

38
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:32,267
exponentially with its distance from the
true answer,

39
00:02:32,267 --> 00:02:35,225
at a rate of epsilon over twice the
sensitivity.

40
00:02:35,545 --> 00:02:40,207
For bit strings, that flips each bit with
probability a little below one half:

41
00:02:40,207 --> 00:02:42,777
Warner's coin again, applied to the answer.

42
00:02:45,617 --> 00:02:47,345
Now the last part of the paper.

43
00:02:47,665 --> 00:02:52,604
Statisticians and data miners traditionally
prefer the non-interactive model:

44
00:02:52,604 --> 00:02:56,966
sanitize the data once, publish it, and let
anyone compute anything.

45
00:02:57,286 --> 00:02:59,142
Can that work under this definition?

46
00:02:59,384 --> 00:03:01,261
The paper proves a striking limit.

47
00:03:01,581 --> 00:03:03,501
Let each row be a string of d bits.

48
00:03:03,821 --> 00:03:06,637
Here is a family of simple counting queries:

49
00:03:06,637 --> 00:03:10,093
give each row its own mask, a subset of bit
positions,

50
00:03:10,093 --> 00:03:14,189
and count the rows with an odd number of
ones inside their mask.

51
00:03:14,509 --> 00:03:18,359
Each query has sensitivity one, so an
interactive curator can

52
00:03:18,359 --> 00:03:22,146
answer any one of them with noise of about
one over epsilon.

53
00:03:22,384 --> 00:03:25,669
But a one-shot release must prepare for all
of them at once.

54
00:03:25,989 --> 00:03:29,773
Theorem 3 shows that for at least two thirds
of these queries,

55
00:03:29,773 --> 00:03:32,581
the release looks almost the same for a
random

56
00:03:32,581 --> 00:03:36,182
database where every row has even parity,
true answer zero,

57
00:03:36,182 --> 00:03:39,173
as for one where every row is odd, true
answer n.

58
00:03:39,493 --> 00:03:43,237
The most extreme difference possible, and it
cannot be seen,

59
00:03:43,237 --> 00:03:46,482
unless the database is exponentially large:
roughly,

60
00:03:46,482 --> 00:03:50,352
every four extra bits per row doubles the
rows you would need.

61
00:03:50,600 --> 00:03:51,642
Pause and ponder:

62
00:03:51,642 --> 00:03:56,424
couldn't an analyst just ask the interactive
curator all of these queries too?

63
00:04:07,267 --> 00:04:08,973
No: the budget would run out.

64
00:04:09,293 --> 00:04:13,693
The curator only has to answer the few
questions someone actually asks,

65
00:04:13,693 --> 00:04:17,781
chosen later. A published release cannot
know which few those will be,

66
00:04:17,781 --> 00:04:20,109
so it must be ready for all of them at once.

67
00:04:20,429 --> 00:04:21,688
That is where it breaks.

68
00:04:21,934 --> 00:04:23,811
The proof idea uses two facts.

69
00:04:24,131 --> 00:04:28,526
Privacy forces the release to treat every
possible row almost alike.

70
00:04:28,846 --> 00:04:33,407
And a random mask splits all rows into two
halves mixed like salt and pepper,

71
00:04:33,407 --> 00:04:36,547
so the even half is like a random poll of
everything.

72
00:04:36,867 --> 00:04:40,110
Then the chain trick: start from completely
random rows,

73
00:04:40,110 --> 00:04:42,542
and swap them one at a time for even ones.

74
00:04:42,862 --> 00:04:46,381
Each swap barely moves the output, and
unless n is huge,

75
00:04:46,381 --> 00:04:48,643
all n swaps together barely move it.

76
00:04:48,963 --> 00:04:53,315
The same goes for odd rows, so both
databases look like random rows.

77
00:04:53,550 --> 00:04:57,603
Warner's coin, randomized response, where
each person scrambles

78
00:04:57,603 --> 00:05:01,784
their own row so nobody holds the raw data,
is even more limited.

79
00:05:02,104 --> 00:05:06,016
Proposition 2: even when every row uses the
same mask,

80
00:05:06,016 --> 00:05:11,812
for most masks it cannot estimate the odd
count unless n is exponentially large.

81
00:05:12,050 --> 00:05:13,628
Careful with the quantifiers.

82
00:05:13,948 --> 00:05:18,258
For any one query known in advance, a one-
shot release can answer it well.

83
00:05:18,578 --> 00:05:22,478
What is impossible, unless the database is
exponentially large,

84
00:05:22,478 --> 00:05:25,511
is one private release that answers most of
them.

85
00:05:25,831 --> 00:05:29,677
The lesson: for broad, flexible accuracy
with strong privacy,

86
00:05:29,677 --> 00:05:31,506
keep the curator in the loop.

87
00:05:33,150 --> 00:05:35,689
Back to our map, to see what grew from this
paper.

88
00:05:36,009 --> 00:05:40,233
The same year, in an invited paper titled
simply Differential Privacy,

89
00:05:40,233 --> 00:05:43,070
Dwork introduced the name the field still
uses.

90
00:05:43,390 --> 00:05:46,868
Another 2006 paper, with Kenthapadi,
McSherry,

91
00:05:46,868 --> 00:05:50,195
Mironov and Naor, added a tiny slack, delta.

92
00:05:50,515 --> 00:05:54,035
Remember the Gaussian whose ratio escaped
the band in the tails?

93
00:05:54,355 --> 00:05:58,131
Delta pays for those rare tails, and lets
the bell curve back in.

94
00:05:58,367 --> 00:06:03,615
Scoring every possible answer became
McSherry and Talwar's exponential mechanism.

95
00:06:03,935 --> 00:06:07,489
The privacy budget grew into a theory of
composition,

96
00:06:07,489 --> 00:06:12,251
with a surprise from Dwork, Rothblum and
Vadhan: allow that tiny delta,

97
00:06:12,251 --> 00:06:17,012
and the total loss of k questions grows only
like the square root of k.

98
00:06:17,250 --> 00:06:21,253
Warner's coin, the model Section four showed
is most limited,

99
00:06:21,253 --> 00:06:24,338
is what we now call local differential
privacy,

100
00:06:24,338 --> 00:06:27,554
used by Google in Chrome and by Apple on
iPhones.

101
00:06:27,874 --> 00:06:29,197
Why the weakest model?

102
00:06:29,517 --> 00:06:33,865
Nobody has to be trusted with the raw data,
and with millions of users,

103
00:06:33,865 --> 00:06:36,621
a few simple statistics can afford the
noise.

104
00:06:36,867 --> 00:06:42,250
In 2016, Abadi and colleagues made it
practical to train deep networks privately:

105
00:06:42,250 --> 00:06:47,566
clip each example's gradient, like our
income cap, which bounds its sensitivity;

106
00:06:47,566 --> 00:06:51,886
add Gaussian noise; and track the budget
over thousands of steps.

107
00:06:52,206 --> 00:06:55,747
If you train models on patient data, this is
your entry point.

108
00:06:56,067 --> 00:07:00,158
A trained network is just another f of x,
and attacks that ask whether

109
00:07:00,158 --> 00:07:04,365
a patient was in the training set are our
subtraction attack, scaled up.

110
00:07:04,600 --> 00:07:06,274
And for the 2020 census,

111
00:07:06,274 --> 00:07:11,714
the US Census Bureau protected its published
tables with differential privacy;

112
00:07:11,714 --> 00:07:15,480
only a few counts, like state populations,
were exact.

113
00:07:15,800 --> 00:07:17,485
But that is a one-shot release.

114
00:07:17,805 --> 00:07:20,429
Pause and ponder: does Section four forbid
it?

115
00:07:29,267 --> 00:07:34,430
No. The Census tuned its release for a fixed
set of tables chosen in advance.

116
00:07:34,750 --> 00:07:37,237
Section four only rules out one release

117
00:07:37,237 --> 00:07:40,808
that is accurate for most of a huge family
of questions.

118
00:07:44,150 --> 00:07:49,270
Let's recap. One: privacy means changing any
one person's row changes

119
00:07:49,270 --> 00:07:54,070
the probability of any output by at most a
factor of e to the epsilon.

120
00:07:54,390 --> 00:07:58,635
Two: a query's sensitivity is the most one
row can change its answer.

121
00:07:58,955 --> 00:08:03,562
Three: Laplace noise with scale sensitivity
over epsilon makes it private,

122
00:08:03,562 --> 00:08:06,550
with error that does not grow with the
database.

123
00:08:06,870 --> 00:08:10,733
Four: one private published table cannot
answer most simple

124
00:08:10,733 --> 00:08:14,465
parity counts unless the database is
exponentially large.

125
00:08:15,100 --> 00:08:18,108
Some questions to test yourself; pause after
each.

126
00:08:18,428 --> 00:08:23,185
First: what is the sensitivity of the
average of n numbers between zero and one?

127
00:08:31,433 --> 00:08:36,284
Second: in a medical imaging dataset, each
patient contributes fifty slices,

128
00:08:36,284 --> 00:08:39,284
and each slice's gradient is clipped to size
C.

129
00:08:39,604 --> 00:08:42,591
How much can one patient change the summed
gradient?

130
00:08:52,833 --> 00:08:56,225
Third: why does the median give the Laplace
mechanism trouble?

131
00:08:56,545 --> 00:08:59,959
Answers, and more exercises, are in the
companion notes.

132
00:09:08,200 --> 00:09:12,103
Next time someone says a dataset is safe
because it is anonymized,

133
00:09:12,103 --> 00:09:14,173
you will know the better questions:

134
00:09:14,173 --> 00:09:17,544
what is the epsilon, and what counts as one
person's row?

